      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my realtor just told me i need to set up an escrow account for the house i'm buying in toronto. whats that actually is and why do i need it for the closing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "An escrow account, in this case, is a separate bank account that holds funds for you, the buyer, until the closing date of the sale. It's like a temporary stash of money, set aside for the various costs associated with buying a house, such as property taxes and insurance. You'll need to deposit funds into this account, and then, on the closing date, the funds will be disbursed to the seller, and you'll receive the keys to your new home.\n\nYou'll typically need to fund the escrow account with a portion of the purchase price, which will cover the upfront costs of property taxes and insurance for the first year. This is a common practice in Toronto, as it helps to ensure that the seller receives the funds they're owed, and it also helps to avoid any last-minute surprises or delays in the closing process.\n\nThink of it like a little \"goblin's nest\" of funds, where the money is safely stored until the right moment to be released. And, just as a goblin might carefully collect and hoard shiny objects, you'll be collecting and setting aside funds for the various costs associated with buying a house. By the time the closing date arrives, you'll be ready to make the",
